{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the children of Gad and the children of Reuben came.</b> It should have preceded the children of Reuben, the firstborn. And you should know that the commentators said that the flocks of the children of Gad were more numerous than those of Reuben, and that is what is written: ״וְלִבְנֵי גָד עָצוּם מְאֹד״, which means, more than those of Reuben, and therefore the children of Gad spoke their words before the children of Reuben the firstborn, because their distress over Reuben's was great. But I say that if so, they certainly did not jump to the front because of their distress, but rather out of the haughtiness of their heart they jumped to speak first, before the children of Reuben the firstborn — for such is the nature of wealth, that it gives a haughty heart to its owners, and a rich commoner jumps to the front and answers insolently, he yields to no man, he gives no honor to the firstborn nor to any persons of true distinction, reckoning that through wealth he is exalted." "And a proof for our words: what was expounded about them in Batei Rabboti (22:8), the verse ״כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב וְלֹא מִמִּדְבַּר הָרִים״ (Psalms 75:7) — not from that which a man goes out from east to west and labors in commerce does he become rich; ״וְלֹא מִמִּדְבַּר הָרִים״ — every 'mountains' in Scripture means actual mountains except this one, which is an expression of exaltation, that a man is not exalted through these things. What does the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He takes possessions from this one and gives to that one, as it is said (there, 75:8): ״כִּי אֱלֹהִים שׁוֹפֵט זֶה יַשְׁפִּיל וְזֶה יָרִים״ etc. Since he attached the exposition of this verse to the children of Gad and Reuben, infer from this that he found in them a trace of arrogance and pride; therefore he expounded this verse about them, to say that not through these is one exalted. And where is it hinted in the verse that they took upon themselves more exaltation than was fitting? Surely, because he found it written that the children of Gad — since they had עָצוּם מְאֹד flocks, more than the children of Reuben — therefore their heart was raised to the point of corruption, and they jumped to speak before the children of Reuben the firstborn. Therefore the verse stated that not through these is one exalted, and he brought a proof for this — that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not desire this, that a man exalt himself on account of his property: ״כִּי אֱלֹהִים שׁוֹפֵט זֶה יַשְׁפִּיל וְזֶה יָרִים״ — in wrath He brings this about: He exalts this one, taking from that one and giving to this one. Is the hand of YHWH too short to give to this one from His own, so that He would not need to take from that one? Rather, surely Elohim did this so that they would fear Him and not raise their hearts because of the abundance of their wealth — by looking at the place from which it was taken from that one, because he was exalting himself in his wealth. For presumably the Holy One, blessed be He, humbles none but the proud, as it is written (Isaiah 40:4): ״וְכָל הַר וְגִבְעָה יִשְׁפָּלוּ״, and therefore He gave this one his property and the exaltation of that one, so that he might take reproof to heart and not raise his heart, lest what happened to his fellow from whom this property was taken happen to him as well." "And that which they expounded 'הָרִים' as an expression of exaltation — their proof is from what is said before this verse (Psalms 75:5–7): ״אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן, אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם תְּדַבְּרוּ בְצַוָּאר עָתָק, כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב וְלֹא מִמִּדְבַּר הָרִים״ — it appears that 'הָרִים' refers back to what was said: 'אַל תָּרִימוּ.' And likewise a proof from what is said after this verse (there, there, 8): ״כִּי אֱלֹהִים שׁוֹפֵט זֶה יַשְׁפִּיל וְזֶה יָרִים״ — infer from this that the essential thrust of all these verses is to warn the owners of wealth not to raise their hearts, for one needs exhortation in a place where there is a full purse." "And that which they expounded 'כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא' as meaning the east — it appears one may explain that this exposition was attached to what the children of Gad and the children of Reuben said: ״כִּי בָאָה נַחֲלָתֵנוּ אֵלֵינוּ מֵעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְרָחָה״ — how did the inheritance come to them? Surely they came to the rest and to the inheritance; so what did they mean by saying that the inheritance came to them? The plain sense of the verse is that they called their property-acquisition and their flocks 'נַחֲלָה,' to say: since we acquired great wealth and flocks in this place in the war against Midian, in addition to the flocks that were already in our hands, therefore it is fitting that the flocks remain in this place from which they came — here they were found and here they shall be. And concerning the flocks that came into their hands in this place they said: ״כִּי בָאָה נַחֲלָתֵנוּ אֵלֵינוּ מֵעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְרָחָה״." "And according to our approach, they alluded to an east wind, for they said that this wealth came to them from מִמּוֹצָא, that is, from the east — from that which a man goes out from east to west in commerce. Therefore he brought this verse to refute their view, as it is said: ״כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא״ etc. And they mentioned עֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן to hint that their view was that wealth comes from a man setting out in ships upon the sea and from traveling overland from east to west; therefore they mentioned עֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן corresponding to one who crosses the paths of the sea in a ship, and they mentioned מִזְרָחָה corresponding to one who goes out from east to west. And the verse refutes both assumptions and says: ״כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב״ — because it is difficult regarding the wording of the midrash, which wrote on this verse: not from that which a man goes out in commerce from east to west does he become rich, and even setting out in a ship, etc. See the wording in the Yalkut on this parashah. And it is difficult — who mentioned a ship? Where is a ship hinted at in the verse? And furthermore it is difficult: why did it formulate 'from east to west' and not also 'from west to east'? For just as it says ״מִמּוֹצָא״, so it says ״וּמִמַּעֲרָב״, for it does not say 'לְמַעֲרָב.'" "Rather, he certainly expounded 'from מוֹצָא' — that is, from east to west; and what is said 'and from the west' means one who departs in a ship on the sea, which is to the west, for the west is called יָמָּה ('seaward') because in that direction is the Great Sea. And since the sons of Gad and Reuben said, 'for our inheritance has come to us from across the Jordan' — that is, from ships — and 'eastward' — that is, from מוֹצָא — therefore he brought against them the verse: 'כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב' — to refute both claims at once." "And as for why they specifically mentioned an east wind for one who goes forth in trade from there — perhaps their reasoning was that an east wind is more beneficial for success in trade than all other winds, for from there is the sunrise that illuminates the earth, for a person should always go forth בְּכִי טוֹב ('in a goodly manner') if the sun has risen upon him favorably, for everything comes from 'the choice of the sun's produce,' and there is no advantage to wealth except 'under the sun.' Therefore the sons of Gad and Reuben said that it is a significant omen to give them an inheritance in the east, so that if they go forth in trade they may go from east to west בְּכִי טוֹב. And this is certainly a correct and clear omen: just as the sun goes forth from there and prospers in its paths, and to its place it aspires to return and come back, so too one who sets out on the road from his home with an east wind at his back will prosper and return to his home. And therefore he used the term מוֹצָא for the east, to tell you that the east is a good sign for one who goes forth from there. And furthermore, the place is across the Jordan and close to it, and the place is suited for travel by ship. And this is a correct and precious allusion.\n\nAnd in the portion of Mishpatim (Exodus 21:1) we explained the verse 'כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא וּמִמַּעֲרָב,' which refers to what is said 'lift not up the horn' — for every haughty person looks: from where did he come, and where is he going? And how can a person take pride in the vapor of the worthless? Should he not understand with his intellect from where his exaltation comes? 'כִּי לֹא מִמּוֹצָא' — the place from which he came forth, for he came from a putrid drop; therefore he mentioned the east in the term 'מִמּוֹצָא.' And 'not from the west' — that is, the time of the sun's setting, as it is said (Ecclesiastes 12:2): 'before the sun be darkened,' for then he goes to a place of dust and rot. 'And not from the wilderness' — from the acquisition of wealth, which resembles a desolate wilderness with no inhabitant; so too the acquisition of wealth dwells in ruins in the wilderness, and it is not a possession attached to a person but is outside of him. From none of these three are there 'mountains' — that is, exaltation, for the word 'mountains' applies to all three of them.\n\nAnd this too is a very precious interpretation according to the intent of the verses that warn against haughtiness of spirit, as it is said: 'lift not up' etc., and he concludes his words with 'this one abases and this one exalts.' And we attached this entire profound derash to the sons of Gad and Reuben, in whom was found the pursuit of wealth to the point that they spurned the coveted land for the sake of wealth — and this small allusion suffices for those who know understanding, and the discerning will hear and add instruction."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {4 "The Aramaic phrase מַאן דְּכַר שְׁמֵהּ is rendered literally as 'who mentioned his name,' i.e., 'who mentioned it at all.' The midrashic source is cited as 'Batei Rabboti (22:8)' in comment 1; here 'the Yalkut on this parashah' is the parallel source being referenced."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:23.318814899Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}